Ectopic Pregnancy
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Unilateral abdominal pain, Vaginal bleeding, Empty uterus on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-013
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, tearing unilateral lower abdominal pain and dizziness with new-onset vaginal bleeding.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and husband
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden onset of severe unilateral abdominal pain, light vaginal bleeding following 6 weeks of amenorrhea, and episodes of syncope. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately evaluate for a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, assess hemodynamic stability, and expedite emergency transport to a surgical facility to prevent fatal hemorrhage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I missed my period by two weeks, and suddenly tonight I had this blinding, sharp pain on my right side and I passed out.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a six-week history of amenorrhoea. One hour ago, she experienced the sudden onset of unilateral sharp, colicky lower abdominal pain localized to the right lower quadrant. This was immediately followed by abnormal vaginal bleeding (dark red spotting). She reports one episode of frank syncope resulting in a fall to the carpet, and currently complains of severe pre-syncope and dizziness upon elevation of her head. She also reports new, deep referred shoulder pain on the right side.
Functional Status: Supine on the floor; completely unable to mobilize due to severe pain and hemodynamic instability.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threatening surgical emergency; high risk of hemorrhagic shock.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 mmHg | HR: 128 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient is markedly pale, diaphoretic, lethargic, and actively guarding her abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a rigid, distended abdomen with exquisite tenderness, guarding, and rebound peritonism localized maximally to the right lower quadrant, but spreading diffusely. Pelvic bimanual examination was deferred due to the severity of the clinical picture and obvious peritonism, but speculum exam (gentle) shows scant dark blood in the vault. Point-of-care urine pregnancy test is immediately and strongly positive.
Environmental Observations: Husband is panicked; clear path to the door for EMS access.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with hemoperitoneum - Status: Critical/Deteriorating
Differentials:

Ruptured Ovarian Cyst - Ruled out due to the positive pregnancy test, severe hemodynamic compromise, and classic referred shoulder pain indicating significant hemoperitoneum.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out by the positive pregnancy test, history of amenorrhoea, and the sudden, catastrophic onset of hypovolemia.

Spontaneous Miscarriage - Unlikely to cause this degree of rigid peritonism, unilateral pain, and shoulder tip pain without massive visible vaginal hemorrhage.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Immediate threat to life)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a state of clinical shock (hypotension, marked tachycardia, syncope) with acute, severe unilateral abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and a positive pregnancy test following a period of amenorrhea. The presence of right shoulder tip pain strongly indicates diaphragmatic irritation from massive hemoperitoneum. This objective clinical evidence confirms a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, an absolute, time-critical surgical emergency. Urgent after-hours presence was required to instantly recognize the catastrophic presentation, initiate emergency medical services (000), position the patient to maximize cerebral perfusion, and provide clinical handover, as survival depends on immediate surgical intervention that could not wait for in-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for priority 1 transport to the nearest tertiary hospital with obstetric surgical capabilities. Placed the patient in the Trendelenburg position (legs elevated) to optimize venous return and cerebral perfusion. Secured a large-bore (16G) peripheral IV cannula while awaiting paramedics, though fluids were withheld pending paramedic arrival to prevent popping early clots (per permissive hypotension protocols).
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain adequate blood flow to the brain, remain absolutely flat on the floor and do not attempt to sit or stand under any circumstances; recognizing the reduction in dizziness will enforce strict compliance. To assist the surgical team, remain strictly nil by mouth (no food or water); understanding this is required for immediate emergency surgery will ensure compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on scene.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover provided to the responding paramedic crew; hospital follow-up required post-discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently under direct medical supervision awaiting immediate ambulance transport for emergency surgery.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a life-threatening surgical emergency requiring immediate operative intervention.
Clinical Course: The ambulance will take you directly to the resuscitation bay at the hospital. You will likely require emergency surgery tonight to stop the internal bleeding.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not move, do not eat or drink anything, and try to take slow, shallow breaths while we wait for the paramedics.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A pregnancy has developed outside of your womb, likely in your fallopian tube, and it has ruptured, causing severe internal bleeding. This is why your blood pressure is so low and you feel faint. The only treatment is immediate surgery at the hospital to stop the bleeding and remove the damaged tissue.
Actionable Steps: Lie completely still. Do not drink any water, even if you are thirsty, as you need an empty stomach for the anesthetic.

